1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,710
Meet Maya.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:03,490
She lives in Northern Virginia,

3
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:05,520
and this is her electric bill.

4
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,697
About about a hundred and fifty dollars

5
00:00:08,697 --> 00:00:09,640
a month.

6
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,650
Now look at her new neighbors.

7
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:12,743
Giant windowless buildings,

8
00:00:12,743 --> 00:00:13,678
packed with AI chips,

9
00:00:13,678 --> 00:00:15,070
humming day and night.

10
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:16,064
This summer,

11
00:00:16,064 --> 00:00:17,794
the grid that powers her street bought

12
00:00:17,794 --> 00:00:20,390
its future electricity at the highest

13
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:21,882
price the rules allow,

14
00:00:21,882 --> 00:00:23,780
and it still came up short.

15
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:25,779
The grid's own watchdog says those

16
00:00:25,779 --> 00:00:28,400
buildings are a big reason why.

17
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,400
So who really pays for the AI

18
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:31,210
boom?

19
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,550
The tech giants...

20
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:33,833
or Maya?

21
00:00:34,283 --> 00:00:41,200
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

22
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:55,190
Here's what those buildings eat.

23
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:58,352
A data center turns electricity into

24
00:00:58,352 --> 00:00:59,026
answers,

25
00:00:59,026 --> 00:00:59,700
and heat,

26
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,220
around the clock.

27
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:03,376
Government researchers at Berkeley Lab

28
00:01:03,376 --> 00:01:04,760
measured it.

29
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:06,036
In their latest count,

30
00:01:06,036 --> 00:01:08,852
America's data centers were already using

31
00:01:08,852 --> 00:01:11,466
as much electricity as about seventeen

32
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:12,870
million homes.

33
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,231
And their forecast says that could nearly

34
00:01:15,231 --> 00:01:15,884
double,

35
00:01:15,884 --> 00:01:17,211
or even triple,

36
00:01:17,211 --> 00:01:19,387
before the decade is out.

37
00:01:19,650 --> 00:01:20,610
Maya doesn't hate AI.

38
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:24,550
Her kids use it for homework.

39
00:01:24,550 --> 00:01:26,910
She uses it to plan dinners.

40
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:30,063
But every one of those questions travels

41
00:01:30,063 --> 00:01:32,163
to a building like this one,

42
00:01:32,163 --> 00:01:34,555
and that building pulls power from the

43
00:01:34,555 --> 00:01:36,122
same grid as her fridge,

44
00:01:36,122 --> 00:01:39,380
her air conditioner and her porch light.

45
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:42,360
Which raises a simple question.

46
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:44,268
When the grid has to grow for

47
00:01:44,268 --> 00:01:44,992
the giants,

48
00:01:44,992 --> 00:01:47,090
who pays for the growing?

49
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:49,140
Let me draw it.

50
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:51,185
Each little house is one home's

51
00:01:51,185 --> 00:01:53,700
electricity for a year.

52
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:54,996
Stack them up,

53
00:01:54,996 --> 00:01:57,909
and today's data centers already make a

54
00:01:57,909 --> 00:02:00,450
tower the size of a big state.

55
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:02,940
Now add the forecast.

56
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:04,626
That's our arithmetic,

57
00:02:04,626 --> 00:02:05,334
not theirs,

58
00:02:05,334 --> 00:02:07,400
but it shows the scale.

59
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:10,350
AI isn't one more appliance.

60
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:12,371
It's like a whole new country of

61
00:02:12,371 --> 00:02:13,022
homes,

62
00:02:13,022 --> 00:02:15,867
plugging into the same wires as Maya.

63
00:02:16,117 --> 00:02:18,517
Grid operators see it coming.

64
00:02:18,697 --> 00:02:20,673
PJM runs the grid for Maya's state

65
00:02:20,673 --> 00:02:22,967
and several others.

66
00:02:23,027 --> 00:02:24,234
A few years ago,

67
00:02:24,234 --> 00:02:26,280
it expected demand on the hottest

68
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:28,517
afternoons to barely grow.

69
00:02:28,777 --> 00:02:30,833
Now it expects it to grow about

70
00:02:30,833 --> 00:02:32,181
twelve times as fast,

71
00:02:32,181 --> 00:02:34,837
mainly because of data centers.

72
00:02:34,977 --> 00:02:36,234
These are forecasts,

73
00:02:36,234 --> 00:02:38,151
and some of these projects will never

74
00:02:38,151 --> 00:02:39,137
be built.

75
00:02:39,137 --> 00:02:40,437
Remember that.

76
00:02:40,447 --> 00:02:42,630
It turns out to matter a lot.

77
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:49,894
So let's follow Maya's bill.

78
00:02:50,044 --> 00:02:52,036
The biggest slice pays for the energy

79
00:02:52,036 --> 00:02:52,934
itself.

80
00:02:52,934 --> 00:02:54,966
Another pays for capacity,

81
00:02:54,966 --> 00:02:56,812
power plants paid to promise they'll show

82
00:02:56,812 --> 00:02:59,054
up on the hottest afternoon.

83
00:02:59,234 --> 00:03:01,227
Another pays for transmission,

84
00:03:01,227 --> 00:03:03,394
the big high-voltage lines.

85
00:03:03,544 --> 00:03:05,496
The rest pays for the poles and

86
00:03:05,496 --> 00:03:06,936
wires on her street,

87
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:08,024
plus fees.

88
00:03:08,114 --> 00:03:10,264
And data centers can push on every

89
00:03:10,264 --> 00:03:11,841
one of those slices.

90
00:03:12,084 --> 00:03:13,002
Start upstream,

91
00:03:13,002 --> 00:03:15,098
in the wholesale market,

92
00:03:15,098 --> 00:03:17,688
where utilities buy power before it

93
00:03:17,688 --> 00:03:18,994
reaches Maya.

94
00:03:19,164 --> 00:03:20,482
So far this year,

95
00:03:20,482 --> 00:03:23,441
the all-in wholesale cost in PJM is

96
00:03:23,441 --> 00:03:25,924
up about fifty percent.

97
00:03:26,184 --> 00:03:28,539
A brutal winter storm explains part of

98
00:03:28,539 --> 00:03:29,354
that.

99
00:03:29,354 --> 00:03:31,879
But the capacity slice alone roughly

100
00:03:31,879 --> 00:03:32,938
tripled.

101
00:03:33,184 --> 00:03:36,084
So the grid's watchdog asked a sharper

102
00:03:36,084 --> 00:03:37,264
question.

103
00:03:37,694 --> 00:03:39,472
What would that cost have been without

104
00:03:39,472 --> 00:03:41,184
the data centers?

105
00:03:41,484 --> 00:03:43,416
Put its estimate in Maya's terms,

106
00:03:43,416 --> 00:03:45,587
and if every cent were passed straight

107
00:03:45,587 --> 00:03:46,433
through to her,

108
00:03:46,433 --> 00:03:49,204
that's about eleven dollars a month.

109
00:03:49,444 --> 00:03:51,824
It usually isn't passed through in full,

110
00:03:51,824 --> 00:03:53,734
and utilities hedge.

111
00:03:53,844 --> 00:03:55,522
But it isn't pennies.

112
00:03:55,784 --> 00:03:58,478
How that capacity auction works,

113
00:03:58,478 --> 00:04:00,629
and why its price first jumped,

114
00:04:00,629 --> 00:04:02,806
is the story we told in Why

115
00:04:02,806 --> 00:04:06,944
America's Electricity Bills Are Rising.

116
00:04:07,134 --> 00:04:09,094
Here's what happened next.

117
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:14,826
Every year,

118
00:04:14,826 --> 00:04:16,363
PJM holds an auction,

119
00:04:16,363 --> 00:04:18,707
paying power plants to promise they'll be

120
00:04:18,707 --> 00:04:21,130
there when the grid needs them most.

121
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:22,677
A few years ago,

122
00:04:22,677 --> 00:04:24,840
that promise was a bargain.

123
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:25,869
This July,

124
00:04:25,869 --> 00:04:28,290
it cleared at the maximum the rules

125
00:04:28,290 --> 00:04:28,929
allow,

126
00:04:28,929 --> 00:04:31,510
more than eleven times higher.

127
00:04:31,510 --> 00:04:33,496
And that maximum is a cap,

128
00:04:33,496 --> 00:04:35,036
set with the governors,

129
00:04:35,036 --> 00:04:37,421
to stop it going even higher.

130
00:04:37,684 --> 00:04:40,254
Think of it like concert tickets.

131
00:04:40,304 --> 00:04:42,424
The seats are power plants.

132
00:04:42,424 --> 00:04:44,032
The crowd is everyone who needs

133
00:04:44,032 --> 00:04:46,443
electricity on the hottest day of the

134
00:04:46,443 --> 00:04:47,244
year.

135
00:04:47,514 --> 00:04:49,845
For years the crowd barely grew,

136
00:04:49,845 --> 00:04:51,754
and tickets were cheap.

137
00:04:51,894 --> 00:04:53,693
Then a new group showed up at

138
00:04:53,693 --> 00:04:54,795
the box office,

139
00:04:54,795 --> 00:04:57,044
with very deep pockets and a very

140
00:04:57,044 --> 00:04:58,544
big appetite.

141
00:04:58,704 --> 00:05:00,612
When the venue can't add seats fast

142
00:05:00,612 --> 00:05:01,402
enough,

143
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:03,855
the price goes up for everyone in

144
00:05:03,855 --> 00:05:04,727
line.

145
00:05:04,984 --> 00:05:06,805
And even at the cap,

146
00:05:06,805 --> 00:05:08,917
the auction came up short of what

147
00:05:08,917 --> 00:05:10,764
PJM says it needs to keep the

148
00:05:10,764 --> 00:05:12,684
lights reliably on.

149
00:05:12,724 --> 00:05:14,667
It's the second auction in a row

150
00:05:14,667 --> 00:05:16,373
where the whole grid fell short,

151
00:05:16,373 --> 00:05:18,484
a first in its history.

152
00:05:18,714 --> 00:05:21,471
Demand is arriving faster than steel and

153
00:05:21,471 --> 00:05:22,572
concrete.

154
00:05:22,817 --> 00:05:26,007
So how much of this is AI?

155
00:05:26,127 --> 00:05:27,317
The market monitor estimates that across

156
00:05:27,317 --> 00:05:28,041
its last several auctions,

157
00:05:28,041 --> 00:05:29,048
data centers were behind almost half of

158
00:05:29,048 --> 00:05:30,017
the capacity bill.

159
00:05:30,017 --> 00:05:31,884
And that bill is shared by everyone

160
00:05:31,884 --> 00:05:33,977
who uses power at the peak.

161
00:05:34,477 --> 00:05:38,582
Including Maya.

162
00:05:38,817 --> 00:05:42,317
The monitor's proposed fix is blunt.

163
00:05:42,317 --> 00:05:44,187
Take the data centers out of the

164
00:05:44,187 --> 00:05:45,205
shared auction,

165
00:05:45,205 --> 00:05:47,467
and make them buy their own power.

166
00:05:47,807 --> 00:05:49,477
PJM is trying other routes,

167
00:05:49,477 --> 00:05:51,591
like letting big new users connect if

168
00:05:51,591 --> 00:05:53,282
they agree to cut back when the

169
00:05:53,282 --> 00:05:54,727
grid is stressed.

170
00:05:54,847 --> 00:05:55,295
None of it has settled the real

171
00:05:55,295 --> 00:05:55,567
question.

172
00:05:55,567 --> 00:05:57,660
Who pays?

173
00:06:02,117 --> 00:06:03,847
Now the wires.

174
00:06:03,927 --> 00:06:05,356
Maya's utility,

175
00:06:05,356 --> 00:06:06,301
Dominion,

176
00:06:06,301 --> 00:06:08,481
and its neighbor AEP have filed plans

177
00:06:08,481 --> 00:06:10,418
to spend more than a hundred and

178
00:06:10,418 --> 00:06:12,840
thirty billion dollars by the end of

179
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:14,167
the decade.

180
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:15,288
New lines,

181
00:06:15,288 --> 00:06:16,570
new substations,

182
00:06:16,570 --> 00:06:18,217
new power plants.

183
00:06:18,377 --> 00:06:20,667
And both point to the same reason.

184
00:06:20,667 --> 00:06:22,261
Giant new customers,

185
00:06:22,261 --> 00:06:24,753
above all data centers.

186
00:06:25,017 --> 00:06:27,807
Dev builds those lines for a living.

187
00:06:27,807 --> 00:06:28,696
Lately,

188
00:06:28,696 --> 00:06:29,357
he says,

189
00:06:29,357 --> 00:06:31,097
the work never stops.

190
00:06:31,097 --> 00:06:32,136
Steel towers,

191
00:06:32,136 --> 00:06:33,175
thick cables,

192
00:06:33,175 --> 00:06:34,481
new substations,

193
00:06:34,481 --> 00:06:37,412
each one planned years ahead for customers

194
00:06:37,412 --> 00:06:39,877
who may or may not show up.

195
00:06:40,107 --> 00:06:42,786
And every tower he raises goes onto

196
00:06:42,786 --> 00:06:44,326
a ledger that someone,

197
00:06:44,326 --> 00:06:45,196
someday,

198
00:06:45,196 --> 00:06:46,689
has to pay off.

199
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:50,210
Here's the part most people never see.

200
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:51,590
Let me draw it.

201
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:54,652
A regulated utility earns a set return

202
00:06:54,652 --> 00:06:56,070
on what it builds.

203
00:06:56,070 --> 00:06:56,892
Build more,

204
00:06:56,892 --> 00:06:58,910
and its earnings grow.

205
00:06:59,010 --> 00:07:01,163
Then the cost is split among groups

206
00:07:01,163 --> 00:07:02,770
of customers.

207
00:07:02,770 --> 00:07:03,537
Homes,

208
00:07:03,537 --> 00:07:04,040
shops,

209
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:05,072
factories,

210
00:07:05,072 --> 00:07:06,710
data centers.

211
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:08,783
And how it's split is a choice,

212
00:07:08,783 --> 00:07:10,594
made by regulators,

213
00:07:10,594 --> 00:07:12,243
with formulas.

214
00:07:12,484 --> 00:07:15,776
And Virginia's own numbers show why that

215
00:07:15,776 --> 00:07:17,364
choice matters.

216
00:07:17,424 --> 00:07:19,311
In Dominion's latest rate case,

217
00:07:19,311 --> 00:07:21,644
a cost study looked at the customers

218
00:07:21,644 --> 00:07:23,552
who would form the new data center

219
00:07:23,552 --> 00:07:24,364
class.

220
00:07:24,394 --> 00:07:26,263
On the big transmission lines,

221
00:07:26,263 --> 00:07:28,994
they earned the utility about a seventh

222
00:07:28,994 --> 00:07:31,304
of the return that other large customers

223
00:07:31,304 --> 00:07:32,114
did.

224
00:07:32,394 --> 00:07:33,396
In plain English,

225
00:07:33,396 --> 00:07:35,849
the biggest users were paying relatively

226
00:07:35,849 --> 00:07:38,084
little for the biggest lines.

227
00:07:38,334 --> 00:07:40,580
The regulators say they'll look at that

228
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:41,629
split next.

229
00:07:41,884 --> 00:07:43,154
And Maya?

230
00:07:43,204 --> 00:07:45,399
The same decision raised her base rates

231
00:07:45,399 --> 00:07:47,395
by about seven and a half percent

232
00:07:47,395 --> 00:07:48,394
this year.

233
00:07:48,744 --> 00:07:50,894
Not all of that is data centers.

234
00:07:50,894 --> 00:07:51,927
It covers storms,

235
00:07:51,927 --> 00:07:52,961
old equipment,

236
00:07:52,961 --> 00:07:54,344
everything.

237
00:07:54,344 --> 00:07:56,511
But it shows where a building boom

238
00:07:56,511 --> 00:07:58,164
finally lands.

239
00:07:58,164 --> 00:08:00,059
On the monthly bill.

240
00:08:04,517 --> 00:08:07,377
But regulators are pushing back.

241
00:08:07,687 --> 00:08:08,451
In Ohio,

242
00:08:08,451 --> 00:08:11,193
after the local utility paused new data

243
00:08:11,193 --> 00:08:12,936
center deals around Columbus,

244
00:08:12,936 --> 00:08:14,907
a new rule arrived.

245
00:08:14,967 --> 00:08:17,003
Big new data centers must pay for

246
00:08:17,003 --> 00:08:19,040
at least eighty-five percent of the

247
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:20,827
capacity they reserve,

248
00:08:20,827 --> 00:08:22,614
whether they use it or not,

249
00:08:22,614 --> 00:08:23,667
for years.

250
00:08:23,827 --> 00:08:25,043
Walk away early,

251
00:08:25,043 --> 00:08:27,087
and there's an exit fee.

252
00:08:27,350 --> 00:08:30,780
And these fights happen in public.

253
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:33,348
When Virginia decided its rules,

254
00:08:33,348 --> 00:08:35,757
the parties in the room included lawyers

255
00:08:35,757 --> 00:08:36,831
for Amazon,

256
00:08:36,831 --> 00:08:38,342
Google and Microsoft,

257
00:08:38,342 --> 00:08:40,292
the data center industry,

258
00:08:40,292 --> 00:08:42,022
consumer advocates,

259
00:08:42,022 --> 00:08:44,432
and the state's own attorney general's

260
00:08:44,432 --> 00:08:45,470
office.

261
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:47,800
Anyone can read the filings.

262
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:49,413
Most people never do,

263
00:08:49,413 --> 00:08:51,750
which is exactly why the outcome can

264
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:53,752
surprise them on their bill.

265
00:08:54,017 --> 00:08:55,835
Virginia followed,

266
00:08:55,835 --> 00:08:57,680
with a new rate class just for

267
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:58,667
the giants.

268
00:08:58,667 --> 00:09:00,097
Long contracts.

269
00:09:00,097 --> 00:09:01,527
Minimum bills.

270
00:09:01,527 --> 00:09:02,727
Collateral.

271
00:09:02,867 --> 00:09:05,467
Dominion says the goal is simple.

272
00:09:05,467 --> 00:09:07,393
Stop one group of customers from

273
00:09:07,393 --> 00:09:09,678
subsidizing another.

274
00:09:09,917 --> 00:09:12,417
Why does a minimum bill matter so

275
00:09:12,417 --> 00:09:13,267
much?

276
00:09:13,317 --> 00:09:15,995
Picture a utility building a line for

277
00:09:15,995 --> 00:09:18,428
a data center that never shows up,

278
00:09:18,428 --> 00:09:19,991
or that shuts down when the AI

279
00:09:19,991 --> 00:09:21,037
boom cools.

280
00:09:21,197 --> 00:09:22,424
Without a contract,

281
00:09:22,424 --> 00:09:24,713
that line stays in everyone's rates for

282
00:09:24,713 --> 00:09:25,917
decades.

283
00:09:25,917 --> 00:09:27,241
With a minimum bill,

284
00:09:27,241 --> 00:09:29,457
the data center pays either way.

285
00:09:29,457 --> 00:09:31,713
That one clause may decide more about

286
00:09:31,713 --> 00:09:34,319
Maya's future bill than any chip.

287
00:09:34,584 --> 00:09:36,837
And the tech giants are signing their

288
00:09:36,837 --> 00:09:38,564
own power deals.

289
00:09:38,664 --> 00:09:41,102
Microsoft is buying the output of a

290
00:09:41,102 --> 00:09:43,053
reactor being brought back to life at

291
00:09:43,053 --> 00:09:45,604
the old Three Mile Island plant.

292
00:09:45,844 --> 00:09:48,427
Meta signed a long-term deal that keeps

293
00:09:48,427 --> 00:09:50,983
an Illinois nuclear plant running,

294
00:09:50,983 --> 00:09:54,424
its owner says without ratepayer support.

295
00:09:54,714 --> 00:09:57,224
Amazon locked in nuclear power in

296
00:09:57,224 --> 00:10:00,334
Pennsylvania for well over a decade.

297
00:10:00,434 --> 00:10:02,478
Some deals add new power to the

298
00:10:02,478 --> 00:10:03,334
grid.

299
00:10:03,404 --> 00:10:06,895
Others reserve power that already exists.

300
00:10:07,150 --> 00:10:09,880
And there's a stronger counterpoint.

301
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:12,071
A Berkeley Lab study found that in

302
00:10:12,071 --> 00:10:12,925
recent years,

303
00:10:12,925 --> 00:10:15,336
more demand has usually pushed prices

304
00:10:15,336 --> 00:10:15,752
down,

305
00:10:15,752 --> 00:10:16,790
not up.

306
00:10:17,090 --> 00:10:19,223
More customers share the fixed cost of

307
00:10:19,223 --> 00:10:20,250
the grid.

308
00:10:20,250 --> 00:10:22,290
It's not clear that will hold.

309
00:10:22,390 --> 00:10:24,150
But new supply is coming.

310
00:10:24,150 --> 00:10:26,170
The EIA expects a wave of new

311
00:10:26,170 --> 00:10:28,790
solar and wind this year and next.

312
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:30,933
And PJM itself trimmed its near-term

313
00:10:30,933 --> 00:10:31,831
forecast,

314
00:10:31,831 --> 00:10:34,365
after checking data center requests more

315
00:10:34,365 --> 00:10:35,886
carefully.

316
00:10:40,350 --> 00:10:41,012
So,

317
00:10:41,012 --> 00:10:44,010
will AI make Maya's bill explode?

318
00:10:44,010 --> 00:10:44,980
Nationally,

319
00:10:44,980 --> 00:10:47,560
the government's own forecast says no.

320
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:50,215
The average price for American homes is

321
00:10:50,215 --> 00:10:52,444
expected to climb about about thirteen

322
00:10:52,444 --> 00:10:54,260
percent over three years.

323
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:55,430
Rising.

324
00:10:55,430 --> 00:10:57,436
Not exploding.

325
00:10:57,684 --> 00:11:00,534
But averages hide the hot spots.

326
00:11:00,604 --> 00:11:02,929
Where data centers cluster and the grid

327
00:11:02,929 --> 00:11:03,604
runs short,

328
00:11:03,604 --> 00:11:06,161
the capacity and wire slices are the

329
00:11:06,161 --> 00:11:07,924
ones that can jump.

330
00:11:08,184 --> 00:11:10,434
Whether they land on Maya comes down

331
00:11:10,434 --> 00:11:11,784
to three dials.

332
00:11:11,894 --> 00:11:12,344
First,

333
00:11:12,344 --> 00:11:14,928
do the giants sign minimum-bill contracts,

334
00:11:14,928 --> 00:11:17,424
so they carry their own grid costs?

335
00:11:17,544 --> 00:11:18,237
Second,

336
00:11:18,237 --> 00:11:20,387
does new supply arrive as fast as

337
00:11:20,387 --> 00:11:21,704
new demand?

338
00:11:21,974 --> 00:11:22,395
Third,

339
00:11:22,395 --> 00:11:24,608
how do regulators split the cost of

340
00:11:24,608 --> 00:11:26,094
shared lines?

341
00:11:26,174 --> 00:11:27,784
Turn them all the right way,

342
00:11:27,784 --> 00:11:30,484
and growth can even spread costs thinner.

343
00:11:30,664 --> 00:11:32,115
Turn them the other way,

344
00:11:32,115 --> 00:11:34,008
and households help pay for the AI

345
00:11:34,008 --> 00:11:34,818
boom.

346
00:11:35,084 --> 00:11:35,738
For Maya,

347
00:11:35,738 --> 00:11:37,766
that means the most important part of

348
00:11:37,766 --> 00:11:39,914
her bill isn't printed on it.

349
00:11:39,914 --> 00:11:42,062
It's the contract the data center down

350
00:11:42,062 --> 00:11:43,114
the road signed,

351
00:11:43,114 --> 00:11:44,144
or didn't.

352
00:11:44,184 --> 00:11:46,159
It's whether the new power plants arrive

353
00:11:46,159 --> 00:11:47,074
on time.

354
00:11:47,074 --> 00:11:48,850
And it's a formula in a rate

355
00:11:48,850 --> 00:11:51,424
case she has probably never heard of.

356
00:11:51,684 --> 00:11:55,094
The AI boom's power bill is real.

357
00:11:55,114 --> 00:11:57,366
It's measured in billions of dollars and

358
00:11:57,366 --> 00:11:59,194
in whole power plants.

359
00:11:59,214 --> 00:12:01,407
But who pays it isn't decided by

360
00:12:01,407 --> 00:12:02,484
physics.

361
00:12:02,484 --> 00:12:05,107
It's decided in auction rules and rate

362
00:12:05,107 --> 00:12:05,799
cases,

363
00:12:05,799 --> 00:12:08,714
in hearing rooms most people never watch.

364
00:12:08,714 --> 00:12:10,607
And that may be the most useful

365
00:12:10,607 --> 00:12:12,709
thing to know about your next electric

366
00:12:12,709 --> 00:12:13,520
bill.

367
00:12:13,784 --> 00:12:14,444
Next time,

368
00:12:14,444 --> 00:12:17,403
we follow the money behind another hidden

369
00:12:17,403 --> 00:12:19,824
link of the world economy.

370
00:12:20,064 --> 00:12:22,367
This is The Hidden Economics.
